{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 390,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Squarespace for Supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2790,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Half of the universe is filled with expansionist\nalien civilizations, and it’s only a matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7540,
    "dDurationMs": 2309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of time before they’ll reach us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9849,
    "dDurationMs": 1881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, that sounded a little sensationalist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11730,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s also the claim of a recent\nastrophysics paper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15460,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see how they came to that conclusion\nand whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18430,
    "dDurationMs": 6990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we should take it seriously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25420,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We humans have been around for only a blink\nof an eye compared to the 13 billion years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30170,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31830,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even the current age of the universe is\nitself a blink of an eye compared to the amount"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36620,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of time yet to come."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38800,
    "dDurationMs": 4091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stars will continue burning for another hundred\ntrillion years, and the heat death of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42891,
    "dDurationMs": 5189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe is a googol years in the future,\nthat’s 1 with a hundred zeros."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48080,
    "dDurationMs": 4069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From this point of view, we’re living pretty\nmuch at the beginning of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52149,
    "dDurationMs": 3581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the same time, when we look at the sky\nwe see… stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55730,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56730,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not a galactic empire in view."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59850,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see no evidence of other life out there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62260,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which may mean that humanity is super early\nin the history of life in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67220,
    "dDurationMs": 4649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which I guess that’s pretty cool - if you\nthink being first to a party is cool."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71869,
    "dDurationMs": 2281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s also weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74150,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See, we have this thing called the Copernican\nprinciple, which states that we shouldn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78979,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expect to find ourselves in a particularly\nspecial place in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83409,
    "dDurationMs": 4421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Named for Nikolaus Copernicus, who showed\nthat the Earth is NOT the center of the universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87830,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as previously thought."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90000,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We are in fact on a typical planet, orbiting\na typical star in a typical galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94320,
    "dDurationMs": 5409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in principle you can extend this principle\nto time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99729,
    "dDurationMs": 4511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If life can appear for trillions of years,\nthen it’s suspicious that we find ourselves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104240,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the first billions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106850,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course the Copernican principle has to\ncompete with the Anthropic principle, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111620,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states that we can only find ourselves in\na location in the universe capable of forming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116560,
    "dDurationMs": 1489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and supporting observers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118049,
    "dDurationMs": 5321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s why it’s not surprising that we’re\non a planet at all, rather than floating in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123370,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a more typical environment: the freezing void\nbetween the galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128690,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So perhaps there’s also an anthropic reason\nfor us to find ourselves at the “beginning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133740,
    "dDurationMs": 1210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of time”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134950,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe the future history of the universe isn’t\nas hospitable to life after all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140560,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This may sound like a lot of speculation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142230,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, how can we know what the future history\nof the universe will be?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146420,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, believe it or not, it IS possible to\nsay something about the earliness of humanity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152580,
    "dDurationMs": 6950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the future of life just based on our arrival\ndate and the apparent emptiness of the sky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159530,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And today we’re going to talk about a study\nthat tries to do exactly that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163310,
    "dDurationMs": 4649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To hear more about the anthropic and copernican\nprinciples, and how we can use them to calculate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167959,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other surprising things about our universe,\nincluding our expiry date, check out our video"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172989,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the Doomsday argument."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174659,
    "dDurationMs": 5181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today we’re looking at the paper  by\nRobin Hanson, Daniel Martin, Calvin McCarter,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179840,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180840,
    "dDurationMs": 1060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jonathan Paulson."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181900,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First these researchers ask the question:\nhow early is humanity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187500,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They try to answer it building a simple model\nof the emergence and spread of intelligent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192080,
    "dDurationMs": 2140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life in our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194220,
    "dDurationMs": 7299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then they use the answer to propose something\npretty wild: a proposal that gives their model"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201519,
    "dDurationMs": 4241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its name: the grabby aliens model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205760,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you’ll have to wait to hear what the\nproposal actually is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208970,
    "dDurationMs": 3799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, how do you determine our “earliness”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212769,
    "dDurationMs": 5181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You start with a function called the appearance\nrate, which tells us how many  intelligent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217950,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilizations are evolving from primordial\ngoop at a given cosmic time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222409,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Using the appearance rate along with our current\ndate, we can work out our civilization’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228400,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "birth rank: of all civilisations that will\never exist, what proportion came before us?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236000,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If most of have already appeared then we are\nlate and have a high birth rank, while if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241519,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most are still to come then we’re early,\nwith a low birth rank."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245319,
    "dDurationMs": 4571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To figure out the appearance rate function,\nthe researchers start with what we know about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249890,
    "dDurationMs": 6129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when habitable worlds formed in the past or\nwhen they will form in the future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256019,
    "dDurationMs": 5921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that most habitable stars are\nformed in a pretty short period of cosmic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261940,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263520,
    "dDurationMs": 3869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the first couple billion years, the universe\ndidn’t contain enough heavy elements for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267389,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rocky planets to form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269479,
    "dDurationMs": 4371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And around 20 billion years in the future,\nthere won’t be enough interstellar material"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273850,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left to form new stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276000,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Habitable star formation will peak at around\n15 billion years, not so far away from our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280990,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current date."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282660,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are a bunch of other factors that cause\nthis peak, which we go into in our episode"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287240,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on galactic habitable zones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290360,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once a habitable star along with its habitable\nplanets have formed, life needs time to evolve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297120,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it needs lot of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We humans only appeared 4 and a bit billion\nyears after our planet formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303800,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how long does this take on average throughout\nthe cosmos?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306740,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To answer this, the researchers use the hard-steps\nmodel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312370,
    "dDurationMs": 6109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get from primordial goop to intelligent\nlife you need a sequence of fluke events."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318479,
    "dDurationMs": 5131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On Earth that involved the first formation\nof self-replicating cells, the evolution of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323610,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complex Eukariot cells, then multi-cellular\norganisms, the photosynthesis, sexual reproduction,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329870,
    "dDurationMs": 5609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "developing giant brains, and various civilization\nand technology steps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335479,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These events are all possible “hard steps”\n- extremely unlikely per-year, but will eventually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341699,
    "dDurationMs": 3291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happen after waiting a very long time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344990,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The number of hard steps is a big deciding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347740,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "factor in how fast the universe can spawn\nintelligent life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351740,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If there are few hard steps, then civilizations\nemerge soon after their stars are formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356800,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If there are many hard steps, it takes\nmuch longer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360410,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we assume that humanity was not particularly\nlucky, in the sense that we didn’t blast"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365180,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through many more hard steps than chance would\nnormally predict, then it’s possible to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370330,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crudely estimate the number of hard steps\nit typically takes to produce intelligent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375410,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life, like us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377970,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The authors’ best guess is between 4 and\n8, admittedly with some major assumptions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382860,
    "dDurationMs": 6429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It should also be noted that the hard steps\nmodel is simplistic - for example, some steps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389289,
    "dDurationMs": 6321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may be easy, but take a long time anyway,\nlike due to the slow but steady buildup of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395610,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "biological complexity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397350,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still, the hard steps model lets us write\ndown a mathematical function for the appearance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rate of life that depends on a simple parameter\n- the number of hard steps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also lets us say something about humanity’s\nearliness based on the typical number of hard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412000,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "steps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413410,
    "dDurationMs": 5939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The more steps there are in the typical evolutionary\npathway, the earlier humanity must be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419349,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another important part of the story is: what’s\nthe time limit for completing these steps?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424879,
    "dDurationMs": 2911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How long do these habitable planets remain\nhabitable?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427790,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s estimated that the Earth will only\nbe habitable for another 1 billion years,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433380,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after which the sun will be too hot for liquid\nwater to exist on the surface of the planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437960,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So Earth will have 5 billion habitable years\nin total."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443500,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If all habitable planets only last this long,\nthen new civilisations would mostly stop appearing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449190,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "5 billion years after the peak of habitable\nstar formation - which, if you remember, is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454890,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "20 billion years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457360,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this case, it seems like we’re a little\nbit early."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460690,
    "dDurationMs": 4569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, there’s one big consideration that\ntotally changes the picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465259,
    "dDurationMs": 4601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There might be other habitable planets that\nlast a lot longer than the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469860,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "M-type stars, also known as red dwarfs, can\nlive for thousands of times longer than our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476000,
    "dDurationMs": 4569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sun, meaning they could have planets that\nremain habitable for trillions of years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480569,
    "dDurationMs": 5411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it’s possible for life to evolve on these\nplanets, then new civilisations could continue"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485980,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to emerge for trillions of years into the\nfuture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489020,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this case, we are very early."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492150,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The experts are split on whether red dwarfs\nare habitable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496250,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since they’re very dim, orbiting planets\nneed to be very close to the star in order"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500230,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to have liquid water, which may be necessary\nfor life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503670,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But planets closer to their star are more\nlikely to be tidally locked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507560,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This can cause erratic climates and make any\npotential inhabitants vulnerable to solar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512830,
    "dDurationMs": 1070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flares."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513900,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The authors of this paper argue that life\ncould well evolve on red dwarf planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519210,
    "dDurationMs": 3459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, why should all life have the same\nrequirements as Earth life?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522669,
    "dDurationMs": 5161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this isn’t the scientific consensus,\nand notably these researchers are not planetary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527830,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scientists nor astrobiologists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529700,
    "dDurationMs": 6590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there are two main deciding factors of\nour birth rank: the number of hard steps and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536290,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the maximum lifetime of habitable planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538980,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By throwing many combinations of these two\nparameters into their model, the researchers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543620,
    "dDurationMs": 2010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generated this diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545630,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s talk through it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548380,
    "dDurationMs": 8540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each point represents a choice of hard-step-number\nand maximum planet lifetime, and the color"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556920,
    "dDurationMs": 3070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that point tells you our birth rank."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559990,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cold colours mean we’re typical, and hot\ncolours mean we’re early."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565240,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If only short-lived planets like Earth are\nhabitable and there are few hard steps, then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570960,
    "dDurationMs": 5431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we were born at a pretty typical period of\nthe cosmos, about a 25% of civilisations were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576391,
    "dDurationMs": 2299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "born before us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578690,
    "dDurationMs": 4389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If red dwarfs are habitable but it’s easy\nfor life to evolve, we’re in something like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583079,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first 0.1% or even first thousandth of\ncivilisations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587910,
    "dDurationMs": 7609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, if there are both long-lived habitable\nplanets and many hard steps, then virtually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595519,
    "dDurationMs": 5981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all civilization will result from very drawn-out\nevolutionary processes trillions of years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601500,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602500,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There will be a billion billion times more\ncivilisations born after us than before us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608340,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, now we get to the wild proposal of the\npaper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612680,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The researchers ask the question: if red dwarfs\nare habitable and there are many hard steps,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617730,
    "dDurationMs": 6910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is there any other way to explain our apparent\nearliness besides extreme chance?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624640,
    "dDurationMs": 5009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea is to come up with some plausible\nscenario that limits the number of possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629649,
    "dDurationMs": 1091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630740,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilizations, even in the case that we should\nexpect them based on the emergence of habitable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635290,
    "dDurationMs": 2140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worlds and the hard steps model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637430,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The so-called grabby aliens model hypothesises\nthe existence of a cosmic deadline for the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644110,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "birth of new civilisations: some catastrophic\nevent that will happen one day in the future"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649180,
    "dDurationMs": 3899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that prevents any new intelligent life from\nemerging."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653079,
    "dDurationMs": 2481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what could cause this deadline?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655560,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, we could."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657160,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by we I mean all civilizations that emerge\nup to that deadline."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661680,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The model claims that at some point in the\nfuture, all habitable planets will be occupied"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667850,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by rapidly expanding alien civilizations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671630,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No new life has the chance to evolve, because\nevery planet is already occupied by space-fairing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676620,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advanced life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677660,
    "dDurationMs": 4239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To reach this conclusion: the model assumes\nthat every intelligent civilization has some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681899,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chance of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682899,
    "dDurationMs": 5911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becoming a grabby civilisation: a civilisation\nthat expands in every direction through space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688810,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at close to the speed of light, making visible\nchanges wherever they go, and that they stick"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694320,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695320,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a long time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696870,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we look at life on Earth, it seems that\nlife has a common tendency to aggressively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701740,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expand into new territories and niches."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705200,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When thinking about our own society today,\nsome might be skeptical that we’re going"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709610,
    "dDurationMs": 4669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be able to expand through the cosmos close\nto the speed of light any time soon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714279,
    "dDurationMs": 4131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even if it takes us a million years to\nwork out how to expand into space and another"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718410,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "million to cross the galaxy at 10% light speed,\nthis delay will have basically no effect on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724470,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmological timescales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726350,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The authors needed to tune the parameters\nof the model to cut off just the right amount"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732790,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of future births of new civilisations to give\nus a typical, run-of-the-mill birth rank."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739310,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If grabby civilizations aren’t common enough,\nthey won’t prevent enough future births"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744670,
    "dDurationMs": 2359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to make us typical rather than early."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747029,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if they’re too common, then we couldn’t\nhave evolved in the first place as Earth would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751980,
    "dDurationMs": 2219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have already been colonised."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754199,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That actually allows us to estimate the number\nof civilizations needed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759399,
    "dDurationMs": 4271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to make sure that humanity isn't too special\nby being too early."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763670,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for that we also need to estimate the\nspeed of grabby expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769500,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We do that with surprisingly useful bit of\ninformation: the fact that we haven’t yet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774560,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seen any evidence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775720,
    "dDurationMs": 3070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of aliens through our telescopes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778790,
    "dDurationMs": 5349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Counterintuitively, this fact implies that\ngrabby expansion is incredibly fast, around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784139,
    "dDurationMs": 1950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a third of the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786089,
    "dDurationMs": 2771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How does this follow?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788860,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Basically, if we could see grabby aliens in\nour skies, the aliens must be moving slowly,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795120,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since they are in our neighborhood but haven’t\nreached us yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799110,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, if we assume they move very fast,\nthen we wouldn’t see them until they basically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803800,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arrive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804800,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805800,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The emptiness of our skies and the constraint\nof making us typical observers is enough to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810990,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "determine all of the parameters of this model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814110,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The researchers then use the model to run\na simulation of the emergence of intelligent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819790,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilizations over time, in which some civilizations\nbecome grabby and spread through the universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826170,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ending with a universe totally full of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830050,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The simulation tells us all sorts of weird\nand surprising things about how space is populated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835560,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least, given the assumptions of this model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838750,
    "dDurationMs": 7250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It tells us that the colonization of the universe\nhas already begun: in fact, around half of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846000,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the volume of the universe is currently colonised."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849350,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It predicts that only around one in a thousand\nintelligent civilisations eventually become"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854120,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space-faring and spread through the cosmos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856440,
    "dDurationMs": 4671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This could be because most civilisations destroy\nthemselves before they get the chance, or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861111,
    "dDurationMs": 1978,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "choose not to take to the skies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863089,
    "dDurationMs": 6211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally: it tells us roughly when we should\nexpect to meet those grabby aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869300,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It gives us a best-guess waiting time of around\nhalf a billion years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873860,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t expect to meet any aliens any time\nsoon, even if they do fill half the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879490,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I guess they’re not in the Milky Way just\nyet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882040,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m not going to say whether the conclusions\nof this paper are right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885250,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember, this is a model, and it’s conclusions\nare only as good as the assumptions behind"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889589,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890589,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this paper is a nice example of how we\ncan figure out a lot given very little - in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895880,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this case, given literally nothing, at least\nin terms of our observation of the existence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900410,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of other civilizations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902430,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if these authors are right - and that’s\na big big if - then it may be that our empty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907210,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sky is one piece of evidence for a universe\nfilled with aliens rushing in to grab this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913750,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rare remaining patch of empty space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917730,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Squarespace for Supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920870,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Squarespace is a website building and hosting\ncompany."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923200,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With Squarespace, you can connect with your\naudience and generate revenue through gated,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927700,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "members-only content."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929000,
    "dDurationMs": 5399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Users can manage members, send email communications\nand leverage audience insights all in one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934399,
    "dDurationMs": 1240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "platform."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935639,
    "dDurationMs": 4521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But one of their most popular features is\ntheir collection of website templates that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940160,
    "dDurationMs": 2010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will help you run a business."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942170,
    "dDurationMs": 5169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whether selling merchandise, booking appointments,\nor collecting reservations for a restaurant,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947339,
    "dDurationMs": 4381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Squarespace has a suite of tools that will\nhelp you run an e-commerce site that looks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951720,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "great on desktop or mobile."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954100,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Go to Squarespace.com for a free trial."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957620,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are a few ways to support this show,\nif you’re inclined."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962200,
    "dDurationMs": 4069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One is to check out our merch store where\nyou can pick up items printed with stuff we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966269,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tend to say on this show."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968649,
    "dDurationMs": 4591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And because we only ever say profound, witty\nthings, that place is a goldmine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973240,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another way to support us is on Patreon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975790,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even signing up for a couple of dollars a\nmonth really helps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979000,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Links to Patreon and the merch store in the\ndescription."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982470,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now today I want to give an extra special\nPatreon shoutout goes to Gautam Shine, who’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987740,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supporting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988740,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us at the big bang level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989740,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gautam, if the paper we just discussed is\ncorrect,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993670,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then humanity is early and this universe has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996880,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "countless trillions of years of life and civilization\nahead of us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001720,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While it’s daunting to think about producing\nPBS Space Time for that long, your help makes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006890,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it more possible that we’ll eventually be\nsyndicated across several intergalactic empires."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012279,
    "dDurationMs": 6990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On behalf of both our present and our far-future\nuploaded or brain-in-vat teams, we thank you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019269,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today’s comment responses are for the episode\non the 2022 Nobel prize, which went to three"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023529,
    "dDurationMs": 2751,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scientists for their work on quantum entanglement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026280,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s get into it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028010,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maltava, Yavor Tch and zomgthisisawesomelol\npoint out that Einstein was referring to wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033970,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse when he said \"spooky action at a\ndistance\" not quantum entanglement as is commonly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039300,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "claimed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040300,
    "dDurationMs": 3070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, yes and no."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043370,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein was referring to general wavefunction\ncollapse, in which the wavefunction appears"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048700,
    "dDurationMs": 5359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to change everywhere at the instant a measurement\nis made, in apparent violation of relativity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054059,
    "dDurationMs": 3401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the speed of light limit on causal influence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057460,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein then got together with Boris Podolsky\nand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060140,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nathan Rosen and basically discovered quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063340,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entanglement in an effort to disprove wavefunction\ncollapse through a reductio ad absurdum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069810,
    "dDurationMs": 7570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He showed that instant collapse of entangle\nwavefunctions led to crazy FTL-like effects,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077380,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so thought it couldn’t be real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080570,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course we now know these effects to be\nvery real features of entanglement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084530,
    "dDurationMs": 5509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, “spooky action at a distance” does\nrefer to wavefunction collapse, including"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090039,
    "dDurationMs": 3241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the wavefunction collapse of entangled\nparticles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093280,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Benjamin Shropshire asks if anyone has ruled\nout hidden influences traveling from the measurement"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099710,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back in time to the creation of the entangled\nstate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103500,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well not only has no one ruled that out, it’s\nbeen shown that this actually works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108370,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s call transactional quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1111350,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John Wheeler and Richard Feynman were the\nfirst to work this out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114650,
    "dDurationMs": 7010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They imagined waves traveling from both cause\nto effect and from effect to cause."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121660,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If there are multiple possible effects, then\nthe cause gets to choose between them by picking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1127750,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the time traveling signals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130270,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However it turns out that the mathematical\ndescription implied by this interpretation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134580,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is equivalent to standard quantum field theory,\nwith the time-reversed signals corresponding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139480,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to negative frequency waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141890,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sabine Hossenfelder has a recent video going\ninto the details."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1145750,
    "dDurationMs": 2309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the one on retrocausality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148059,
    "dDurationMs": 5881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jose Martinez asks if there’s a problem\nusing a random number generator to decide"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153940,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement direction, considering that true\nrandomness is impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159070,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well there is potentially a problem, yes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1161570,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a super deterministic universe, the not-actually-random\nRNG could be correlated with the event that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168650,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produced the photons, leading to the violation\nof the Bell inequality even if the photon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1173500,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never existed in a superposition of states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1176690,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that really appears to require a pretty\ncomplicated conspiracy of influences connecting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1182580,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1183580,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement device with every potential measurement\nsubject, and I’ve never heard a good description"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1188430,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of how the details work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1190670,
    "dDurationMs": 7930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Radar they plan to dress as a Quantum Entangled\nParticle this Halloween and so doing causing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1198600,
    "dDurationMs": 1319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lots of spooky action"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1199919,
    "dDurationMs": 1691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a distance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1201610,
    "dDurationMs": 5309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This really got me thinking about other physics-themed\nHalloween costumes I could do this year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1206919,
    "dDurationMs": 4601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I considered the ghost particles from our\nrecent standard model lagrangian episode,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1211520,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Schrodinger’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212520,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "half-dead cat, phantom energy, Maxwell’s\ndemon, or Laplaces demon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1217620,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then I figured it out - for my costume\nI entered a quantum superposition of both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1222990,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going and not-going to all of the parties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1225480,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This branch of my wavefunction only remembers\nnot going to any of them, but I assume the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1231620,
    "dDurationMs": 1399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other guy had a really great time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
